\name{leaf_annotation}
\alias{leaf_annotation_rect}
\alias{leaf_annotation_heatmap}
\title{
Categorical and numeric leaf annotation tracks
}
\description{
\strong{\code{leaf_annotation_rect()}:} Append categorical annotation columns matched to leaf names.

\strong{\code{leaf_annotation_heatmap()}:} Append numeric heatmap columns matched to leaf names.
}
\usage{
leaf_annotation_rect(df, legend = "top_right", colors = NULL, width = 16)
leaf_annotation_heatmap(x, legend = "top_right", colors = c("#2166AC", "white",
    "#B2182B"), limits = NULL, width = 16)
}
\arguments{
\item{df}{\strong{\code{leaf_annotation_rect} only:} Required data frame with a \code{name} column and at least one category column. Names must be nonmissing, unique known leaves. Each remaining column produces one track; \code{NA} categories and omitted leaves are left blank.}
\item{legend}{One legend-position string: \code{"top_right"} (default), \code{"top_left"}, \code{"bottom_right"}, \code{"bottom_left"}, or \code{"none"}.}
\item{colors}{\strong{\code{leaf_annotation_rect}:} Default \code{NULL} uses \code{hcl.colors(..., "Dark 3")} per column. Otherwise a category-named color vector shared by columns, or a list named by column containing category-named vectors. Every category must be named, including unused factor levels. \code{NA} mapped colors leave cells blank. An R color name, \code{#RRGGBB} or \code{#RRGGBBAA}; alpha is supported.

\strong{\code{leaf_annotation_heatmap}:} Vector of R colors interpolated into a continuous palette. Default \code{c("#2166AC", "white", "#B2182B")} (blue, white, red). An R color name, \code{#RRGGBB} or \code{#RRGGBBAA}; alpha is supported.}
\item{width}{One finite positive number: width of each annotation column in logical pixels, default 16. This is not the entire track group's width or a relative canvas fraction.}
\item{x}{\strong{\code{leaf_annotation_heatmap} only:} Required nonempty numeric matrix with unique known leaf names in \code{rownames(x)}. Infinite entries are rejected; \code{NA} entries are blank. Column names become track titles; absent column names are replaced by \code{V1}, \code{V2}, and so on. Omitted leaves are blank.}
\item{limits}{\strong{\code{leaf_annotation_heatmap} only:} Two finite numbers in increasing or equal order. Default \code{NULL} uses the range of all nonmissing entries in the entire matrix, or \code{c(0, 1)} for an all-missing matrix. Values outside the limits are clipped; equal limits use the palette midpoint. No automatic standardization or Z-score is applied.}
}
\details{
\subsection{leaf_annotation_rect}{
Factor levels determine category and legend order, including unused levels; other columns use first-occurrence order among nonmissing values. Rows may be in any order. Layers append outward in addition order, with labels between tree and tracks. Track geometry follows the chosen tree layout. Legends sharing a corner are arranged in order and may wrap into columns.
}
\subsection{leaf_annotation_heatmap}{
Rows match leaf names, not row positions. All columns share a single continuous color scale computed from the whole matrix. Repeated layers append outward rather than replacing previous tracks. Each layer has its own scale and legend; use identical \code{colors} and \code{limits} when layers need a common scale.
}
}
\section{Similarities and differences}{
Both functions match by leaf name and append one track per data column. leaf_annotation_rect uses a data-frame name column and category-to-color mappings. leaf_annotation_heatmap uses matrix row names and one continuous numeric scale. Width is per column in logical pixels. Missing leaves or values are blank. Use free_heatmap for a matrix placed independently of the tree.
}
\value{
\describe{
\item{\code{leaf_annotation_rect()}}{A \code{yytree_layer} value; add it to a plot with \code{+}.}
\item{\code{leaf_annotation_heatmap()}}{A \code{yytree_layer} value; add it to a plot with \code{+}.}
}
}
\seealso{
\code{\link{layout}}, \code{\link{leaf_labels}}, \code{\link{free_heatmap}}
}
\examples{
# leaf_annotation_rect()
categories <- data.frame(name = c("C", "A"),
                         group = factor(c("treated", "control")))
p <- yytree("((A:1,B:2):1,C:3);") + leaf_labels() +
  leaf_annotation_rect(categories,
    colors = c(control = "steelblue", treated = "orange"), width = 12)

# leaf_annotation_heatmap()
values <- matrix(c(0, 5, NA, 10), nrow = 2,
                 dimnames = list(c("C", "A"), c("control", "treated")))
p <- yytree("((A:1,B:2):1,C:3);") +
  leaf_annotation_heatmap(values, limits = c(0, 10), legend = "bottom_right")

}
